Vectors let you compute without committing to axes. Write $\mathbf a$ for the position vector of $A$ relative to some origin. The dot product satisfies $\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v=|\mathbf u|\,|\mathbf v|\cos\theta$, so

- $AB\perp CD$ exactly when $(\mathbf b-\mathbf a)\cdot(\mathbf d-\mathbf c)=0$;
- $AB^2=(\mathbf b-\mathbf a)\cdot(\mathbf b-\mathbf a)=|\mathbf a|^2+|\mathbf b|^2-2\,\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b$;
- the centroid is $\mathbf g=\tfrac13(\mathbf a+\mathbf b+\mathbf c)$, because the point two thirds of the way from $A$ to the midpoint $\tfrac12(\mathbf b+\mathbf c)$ is $\mathbf a+\tfrac23\big(\tfrac12(\mathbf b+\mathbf c)-\mathbf a\big)$.

The best choice of origin is often the circumcentre $O$. Then $|\mathbf a|=|\mathbf b|=|\mathbf c|=R$, the circumradius, and many dot products simplify. The next theorem shows how much this buys.

Identify the plane with $\mathbb C$, writing the point $A$ as the complex number $a$. Translation is addition, rotation by $\theta$ about $0$ is multiplication by $e^{i\theta}$, and reflection in the real axis is conjugation $z\mapsto\bar z$. Angles are read off from quotients: the argument of $\dfrac{d-c}{b-a}$ is the angle from direction $AB$ to direction $CD$. Hence

- $AB\parallel CD$ exactly when $\dfrac{d-c}{b-a}$ is real, that is, equal to its own conjugate;
- $AB\perp CD$ exactly when $\dfrac{d-c}{b-a}$ is purely imaginary, that is, equal to minus its conjugate;
- $A,B,C$ are collinear exactly when $\dfrac{c-a}{b-a}$ is real.

Conjugates normally double the number of unknowns. The unit circle removes that cost: if $|a|=1$ then $a\bar a=1$, so $\bar a=1/a$. When the points of a problem lie on one circle, make it the unit circle, and every conjugate of an expression in $a,b,c,\dots$ becomes a rational function of the same letters. A compact way to test whether two nonzero vectors $u,w$ are parallel is to compare $u/\bar u$ with $w/\bar w$: the quotient $u/w$ is real exactly when $u/w=\bar u/\bar w$, i.e. when $u/\bar u=w/\bar w$, and purely imaginary exactly when $u/\bar u=-w/\bar w$.

In the key statement below, the same letters are used in two ways, as is customary: on the left, $ab$ means the line through $a$ and $b$; on the right, $ab$ is the product of two complex numbers.

Let $a,b,c,d$ lie on the unit circle, with $a\ne b$ and $c\ne d$. Then:

1. a point $z$ lies on the line $ab$ if and only if $z+ab\,\bar z=a+b$;
2. $ab\parallel cd \iff ab=cd$;
3. $ab\perp cd \iff ab+cd=0$;
4. the foot of the perpendicular from any point $z$ to the line $ab$ is $\tfrac12\left(a+b+z-ab\,\bar z\right)$.
